WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.360
Film Courage: We talked about your process
for rewriting, let’s talk about rewriting

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:08.490
your own project or a project that you’re
initially hired to write, not someone else’s

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:09.490
first.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.970
So what is your process?

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.080
You open up your computer you sit down, you’re
not really looking at prior pages before that

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.080
time (I assume)?

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.400
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter: Rewriting as
you mean you’ve turned in a draft and they

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.400
gave me notes?

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.520
Film Courage: Yeah and they say “We like
this” but here are some notes and you’re

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.940
going back over from pretty much page one.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:32.619
Mark: Sure…last month we just went through
that and we’ll probably go through that

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:35.010
next month because I just turned in something.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:38.730
But last month the movie that is shooting
now I did rewrites.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:44.030
The first draft was pretty solid the second
draft a few sequences changed because of particular

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:51.309
things and so there’s always a list of notes
specific of what needs to be changed and what’s

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:58.460
not quite working and needs to be finessed
and many things are sometimes technical without

00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:59.699
getting into particulars.

00:00:59.699 --> 00:01:05.089
But how a sequence is done you know it’s
always objects.

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:08.869
Well how would that person get that thing?

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:12.950
In my mind I write it be then there’s the
director’s mind who has to really shoot

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:18.439
the film and so that doesn’t come into play
until the director comes on board.

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:22.849
So then the notes come in from the producer
and then let’s say another producer who

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:26.609
hasn’t chimed in yet and then the director
and then they say “Okay.”

00:01:26.609 --> 00:01:32.029
And the director is look at it hopefully about
how can I shoot this film with the money that

00:01:32.029 --> 00:01:36.740
I have and your mind but I’ve never been
at the locations.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.780
So then a lot of the notes changed “Well
we can only get this kind of place.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.030
We need to tweet for that.”

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.439
And you go “Okay.”

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:45.289
And those are great.

00:01:45.289 --> 00:01:51.569
Or don’t have them have this thing, have
them do (you know, I’m being vague), objects,

00:01:51.569 --> 00:01:52.759
gu, whatever.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:54.039
Things in the script.

00:01:54.039 --> 00:01:58.889
So you can go through with almost a checklist
and go and make the fixes and hopefully like

00:01:58.889 --> 00:02:04.679
I said with a solid treatment it shouldn’t
be like the house of cards implodes because

00:02:04.679 --> 00:02:08.509
that’s not the place to be in development
when you’re like “Oh my gosh, I’ve got

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:10.899
to rewrite this whole thing.”

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:19.250
It’s still solid, A…B…and C still happens,
you get in and you start to…there is a sequence

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:27.120
that changed by its own nature and I agree
totally with the changes because nobody sets

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.200
out to make a bad movie and the changes are
not personal.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.219
They’re like “Oh we thought of it and
it’s terrible.”

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:36.620
Somebody had to think of it, somebody had
to write it down, somebody had to create it

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.849
and then comes the realities of shooting a
movie.

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:44.750
And so my job at that point is to help the
make the movie.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.629
My job is to help the producer and director’s
job make the film.

00:02:48.629 --> 00:02:54.299
I’m not standing in their way, whatever
needs to be changed, it’s changed because

00:02:54.299 --> 00:03:01.319
now we’re moving through development to
a reality phase (not this fantasy).

00:03:01.319 --> 00:03:04.580
Motivations have to track, if they don’t
they say this is kind of weird, would that

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.780
character do this?

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.409
Maybe the other would flip.

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:12.510
You were saying about the strong, keeping
in mind the protagonist, well you gave that

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.730
to this other character, she should be doing
this.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.290
You’re like “Oh, yeah you’re right.”

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.680
It’s just an easy fix because the character
then now is driving the story and it’s happening

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:28.340
to them rather not pushing it off to a co-star
or something.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.959
And it’s very (I don’t want to say methodical)
but it is kind of methodical because after

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:36.859
doing this for so long it’s like “This
is what needs to be done.”

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:42.230
And double-checking, me going back through,
looking at all that, putting on revision mode

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:52.459
so everybody is on the same page, and then
they get it and the nicest thing is to hear…you

00:03:52.459 --> 00:03:58.340
know do a quick polish let’s say a couple
day polish which is fixing little things that

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:04.480
the producer or director comes back and says
“That was a great job.”

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.440
I still get happy, I’m still happy about
hearing this because they don’t have to

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:09.659
say good job.

00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:15.370
You job is you got paid but great job.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:21.120
And on time because if you had take a week
that would push, they are already scouting

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.161
locations, they are doing things.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:23.670
I don’t want to hold up that.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:29.340
I want to get this turned around as fast as
I can also to get it off my plate, to open

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:32.490
up for other work.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:36.900
You want to be a team player (as I say) an
ultimate collaborator at all steps in the

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:37.900
way.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:42.770
And know your and know your place in the bigger
picture and so if go to the set if I’m not

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:44.979
working on the set I’m a guest.

00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:47.000
I’m there, it’s nice.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:52.400
I’ve been on sets that I worked writing,
I’ve also been on sets as a guests where

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.759
the director returned to me and he knew my
first spec how long it took to get it made

00:04:57.759 --> 00:04:59.789
and he would turn to me and say “What did
you think about that take?”

00:04:59.789 --> 00:05:01.569
And I’m like “Are you taking to me?”

00:05:01.569 --> 00:05:03.590
And I’d go “Actually I thought it was
excellent.”

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:04.590
And he’d go “Me too.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:05.590
Moving on.”

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:06.590
And you’re like “Aahhh.”

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:07.949
Film Courage: Angels singing in the background.

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:15.440
Mark: Yeah, that’s the great stuff you work
for and then there are the dark stormy nights

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.169
when you’re in despair and all these others
things.

00:05:21.169 --> 00:05:26.120
Or that movie premiered at the Palm Springs
Film Festival, it opened and so we had a theater

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.900
full of 300 people and I’m sitting with
the stars and one of them I grew up as a kid

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.699
watching, he’s an academy nominee and he
turns over after the movie and he grabs my

00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:37.540
arm and he says “Great job, Mark.”

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:39.750
And I’m like “He…he’s the guy with
the thing.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:40.960
Who is he talking to?

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.440
Oh yeah, I wrote this movie.”

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:47.500
And then we get up and we do the Q&amp;A and someone
asks the star (the lead), they say “You

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:52.229
know in that one scene, I love what you said
to your son it was so amazing.”

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:55.610
And an actor could have gone “Thank you
very much.”

00:05:55.610 --> 00:06:00.419
And the star of the movie said “Well you
know people always say to actors what they

00:06:00.419 --> 00:06:02.130
say is genius.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.130
Without the writer…”

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:06.180
And he put his arm around me and he pulled
me close and he said “There would be nothing

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.180
to say.”

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:08.610
And that was another like “Huh?”

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:13.759
So you have these high of highs but it’s
not always like that.

00:06:13.759 --> 00:06:17.440
Film Courage: Sure but that sound like that
worth doing.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:21.620
Mark: It makes up for all the hard work and
stuff and you want to get to that point where

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:25.620
you’re on the set and you’re an equal
collaborator not just a fanboy going “Oh

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:26.620
my gosh!

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:27.620
Oh yeah I wrote this.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:28.620
You’re right.”

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:33.539
And even more when I’ve had relationships
with the director who said I want you on the

00:06:33.539 --> 00:06:39.650
set just as a good luck piece because we start
making changes, I want somebody to go “Whoah,

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:40.830
whoah, whoah!”

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:44.480
A lot going on on the set and people forget
and things fall through the cracks, well the

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.289
writer is going to go “Hey, hey, hey.

00:06:46.289 --> 00:06:53.189
That line…you might not want to cut it because
it’s going to change things.”

00:06:53.189 --> 00:06:57.289
But also they don’t want writers on the
set because they are changing things and you

00:06:57.289 --> 00:06:59.129
don’t want to be “Aaarghh!”

00:06:59.129 --> 00:07:02.750
Grumbling and walking around angry.

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:08.360
How could you be angry, you’re eating seared
Ahi at craft service and you got paid and

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:12.520
these wonderful actors are elevating your
material.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:14.660
I mean, sounds good to me.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:17.360
Film Courage: Sounds nice to me.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:21.919
What’s day one of a rewrite like for someone
else’s work?

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:26.310
So they’ve given you notes, this is what
the other writer gave us, this is what’s

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:27.310
wrong.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:31.050
Mark: It’s the same…you’re saying it’s
different than my own?

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:35.389
Film Courage: I guess then it is the same
process then&gt;

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:41.879
Mark: Yeah, it’s chipping away, my own rewrites
is based upon notes of people that I trust

00:07:41.879 --> 00:07:43.060
like say a spec.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:46.000
I would give it to a circle of writer friends
who would give me notes.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.660
I’d say “Hhmm…yeah…okay.”

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:57.060
And then go about the rewrite but it’s similar
to when you just check off the checklist and

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:05.379
say okay and get that draft done and push
each draft closer to the final realization

00:08:05.379 --> 00:08:06.639
of the production draft.

00:08:06.639 --> 00:08:12.469
Obviously your spec is a spec, it’s not
a production but in a rewrite my job is to

00:08:12.469 --> 00:08:16.580
push that as quick as I can to get these changes
made and not hold up everything.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:21.370
Film Courage: And you’re doing these changes
in numeric order in terms of the script page?

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.659
Mark: Yeah, I mean they are specific which
I love.

00:08:23.659 --> 00:08:27.639
Other times I’ve had broad strokes like
well which I put in the book [A SCREENWRITER’S

00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:28.639
JOURNEY TO SUCCESS: Tips, tricks and tactics
to survive as a working screenwriter in Hollywood].

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:31.860
Some of the notes are “The emotional highs
have to be higher and the lows have to be

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:32.860
lower.”

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:35.090
“What, what does that mean?”

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:36.360
And then they go “Okay, good luck.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:37.360
See you in a week.”

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:41.260
And you’re like “I don’t know what you’re
talking about?”

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:45.340
And then if you come back and ask too many
questions “Why did we hire you again?

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:47.220
You don’t know what you’re doing?”

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.220
“Yes, I do.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:49.960
You don’t know what you’re doing?”

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.290
Not all producers know how to talk to writers
properly.

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:56.700
They think they do and the really good ones
do where they like really get in there because

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.970
some of them are writers as well.

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:02.450
But the ones that just paint a broad stroke
and then “See you later and they’re busy

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:06.880
with their other projects and you have to
learn how to filter that and go “The emotional

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:08.190
highs and lows, what page?

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:11.130
What character, I don’t understand?”

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.950
And they go “You don’t understand?

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:13.950
Okay.”

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:15.100
And they pull the chute and you’re gone.

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:19.610
Film Courage: Is this LEAVING LAS VEGAS low,
MARY POPPINS high?

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:22.880
Mark: Exactly, it’s like what are we doing?

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:30.010
But the page specific notes, which then become
scene specific notes once the scene numbers

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:35.420
happen they are like “In this scene X, Y
and Z.”

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:36.490
And you’re okay, blah and this dialogue
here blah.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:43.630
And so you just go in an do it, change, change,
and change and turn that draft in and hope

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:46.210
that’s closer and closer and closer.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.700
What you don’t want is to be farther and
farther away.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:50.700
That’s when you bring somebody else in.

